00:05:40,261 --> 00:05:42,180
bus circuit records are in.

110
00:05:42,181 --> 00:05:44,520
The Blackhawk helicopter crew includes two pilots.

111
00:05:46,720 --> 00:05:49,840
To fly it, the pilots must use both hands and feet.

112
00:05:50,640 --> 00:05:53,760
The helicopter's superior maneuverability comes with complexity.

113
00:05:54,980 --> 00:05:59,480
The key to understanding how to fly a Blackhawk, or any helicopter, starts at the hub.

114
00:06:00,940 --> 00:06:03,920
The hub is a central portion of the rotor system.

115
00:06:03,921 --> 00:06:07,400
It's the part where all four rotor blades hook together.

116
00:06:07,401 --> 00:06:14,360
The hub, or rotor disc, tilts to give the helicopter directional flight and is controlled by the cyclic.

117
00:06:16,140 --> 00:06:17,727
This is the cyclic.

118
00:06:17,728 --> 00:06:24,660
This provides us directional control, either at a hover or in a cruise flight mode.

119
00:06:25,140 --> 00:06:30,620
With a cyclic, I'm actually tilting the rotor disc in the direction that I'm pushing the cyclic.

120
00:06:30,621 --> 00:06:34,660
So therefore, if I'm at a hover and I move the cyclic to the right,

121
00:06:34,840 --> 00:06:37,480
then the aircraft rotor disc is going to tilt to the right,

122
00:06:37,580 --> 00:06:38,960
and I'm going to drift to the right.

123
00:06:39,700 --> 00:06:43,540
If I push the cyclic forward, the rotor system is going to tilt forward,

124
00:06:43,640 --> 00:06:45,400
the aircraft is going to go into a descent,

125
00:06:45,660 --> 00:06:49,260
and I look outside and the houses on the ground are actually getting bigger

126
00:06:49,261 --> 00:06:50,920
because I'm getting closer to the ground.

127
00:06:51,680 --> 00:06:53,960
To control the lift or thrust of the helicopter,

128
00:06:54,440 --> 00:06:56,160
the pilot uses the collective.

129
00:06:57,240 --> 00:07:00,380
The flight control that I have in my left hand here is called the collective.

130
00:07:00,620 --> 00:07:04,620
And it's called the collective because it collectively puts

131
00:07:04,621 --> 00:07:08,260
blade pitch angle into all four rotor blades at the same time.

132
00:07:08,261 --> 00:07:10,120
The more pitch I want in the rotor blade,

133
00:07:10,121 --> 00:07:12,460
the higher I pull the collective.

134
00:07:12,461 --> 00:07:15,260
The pitch angle determines how fast the helicopter will lift

135
00:07:15,261 --> 00:07:20,380
off the ground in a hover or how fast it will fly in flight.

136
00:07:20,381 --> 00:07:23,180
A zero angle will produce zero lift.

137
00:07:23,181 --> 00:07:26,680
As the pitch increases, the blades will push more arid downward,

138
00:07:26,681 --> 00:07:29,380
resulting in greater lift.

139
00:07:29,381 --> 00:07:32,220
Once in the air, the pilot pushes the cyclic forward

140
00:07:32,320 --> 00:07:36,020
to start the helicopter in a forward direction.

141
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:38,820
RPA 069, we'll just slow down here in the orbit.

142
00:07:38,920 --> 00:07:40,620
Just let you catch up to us.

143
00:07:40,720 --> 00:07:42,320
As the pilot flies the aircraft,

144
00:07:42,420 --> 00:07:45,020
he uses the tail rotor to keep the nose of the helicopter

145
00:07:45,120 --> 00:07:47,540
pointing forward.

146
00:07:47,640 --> 00:07:49,140
The purpose of that tail rotor is

147
00:07:49,240 --> 00:07:52,240
to provide thrust in the opposite direction

148
00:07:52,340 --> 00:07:54,680
of the main rotor turning so that we can maintain

149
00:07:54,780 --> 00:07:56,680
our direction on flight.

150
00:07:56,780 --> 00:07:58,280
Since the main rotor system turns

151
00:07:58,281 --> 00:08:01,180
counterclockwise, there's an equal and opposite reaction

152
00:08:01,280 --> 00:08:03,860
that forces the fuselage clockwise.

153
00:08:03,960 --> 00:08:06,580
Tail rotor counters that.

154
00:08:06,680 --> 00:08:08,320
It works the same way as the main rotor

155
00:08:08,420 --> 00:08:10,820
in that its blades increase pitch to gain thrust.

156
00:08:13,360 --> 00:08:15,500
To maintain a pitch that will keep the helicopter moving

157
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:20,300
forward, the pilot uses his feet to control the tail rotor.

158
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:22,500
If I push left pedal, then that causes

159
00:08:22,600 --> 00:08:26,140
the nose of the helicopter to turn left.

160
00:08:26,141 --> 00:08:28,780
And then I used counter pressure on the opposite pedal

161
00:08:28,781 --> 00:08:30,220
to stop that motion.

162
00:08:33,420 --> 00:08:35,480
The Black Hawk has two crew chiefs

