When you're operating in environments

256
00:13:18,860 --> 00:13:20,920
where it's very difficult to travel over roads,

257
00:13:21,020 --> 00:13:24,060
especially in the environments where we've seen IEDs,

258
00:13:24,160 --> 00:13:27,860
explosive devices, roadside bombs become a major issue,

259
00:13:27,960 --> 00:13:30,360
we're able to move a lot of equipment over the air

260
00:13:30,460 --> 00:13:33,440
without that threat to worry about.

261
00:13:33,540 --> 00:13:35,440
Nevertheless, in these environments,

262
00:13:35,540 --> 00:13:38,940
there's a real threat of being shot down.

263
00:13:39,040 --> 00:13:41,540
If the worst happens and the engines fail,

264
00:13:41,640 --> 00:13:44,920
CH-53 pilots rely on an aerodynamic phenomenon

265
00:13:44,921 --> 00:13:49,160
to all helicopters, called autorotation.

266
00:13:49,161 --> 00:13:52,120
As the helicopter falls, the upward flow of air

267
00:13:52,121 --> 00:13:53,720
through the rotor provides enough force

268
00:13:53,721 --> 00:13:57,000
to keep the blades turning, much like a pinwheel.

269
00:13:57,001 --> 00:13:59,040
The pilot will lower the collective,

270
00:13:59,041 --> 00:14:01,680
decreasing the angle of the blades.

271
00:14:01,681 --> 00:14:04,040
This creates just enough lift to slow the descent

272
00:14:04,041 --> 00:14:07,040
of the helicopter, and in ideal conditions,

273
00:14:07,041 --> 00:14:09,880
allows the pilot to land safely.

274
00:14:09,881 --> 00:14:13,040
The problem with this aircraft is it's very heavy.

275
00:14:13,041 --> 00:14:15,840
It falls like a rock if the engines are out.

276
00:14:15,940 --> 00:14:17,980
And if you get that one shot, it's

277
00:14:18,080 --> 00:14:20,840
going to be a hard landing no matter what you do.

278
00:14:20,940 --> 00:14:24,420
But while the super stallion carries the heaviest loads,

279
00:14:24,520 --> 00:14:28,420
and the Apache can rain death on the enemy,

280
00:14:28,520 --> 00:14:31,760
it's the helicopter's ability to hover over a sinking ship

281
00:14:31,860 --> 00:14:33,920
or a survivor floating in the ocean

282
00:14:34,020 --> 00:14:35,800
that makes it an essential rescue

283
00:14:35,900 --> 00:14:39,500
tool for the men and women of the US Coast Guard.

284
00:14:39,600 --> 00:14:42,240
And it's this capability that sets it apart from the plane

285
00:14:42,241 --> 00:14:44,540
from the planes and boats the Coast Guard uses

286
00:14:44,541 --> 00:14:46,740
in conjunction with the helicopter.

287
00:14:46,741 --> 00:14:48,780
Well, the helicopter allows the Coast Guard

288
00:14:48,781 --> 00:14:51,040
to do the rescue part of search and rescue.

289
00:14:51,041 --> 00:14:55,080
We could always go find somebody with some other kind of asset,

290
00:14:55,081 --> 00:14:57,580
but the helicopter's really what lets us go out,

291
00:14:57,581 --> 00:14:59,120
get somebody, and bring them home.

292
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,200
For over 75 years,

293
00:15:04,201 --> 00:15:06,200
the helicopter has been an essential tool

294
00:15:06,201 --> 00:15:10,440
in the Coast Guard's mission to save lives on the high seas.

295
00:15:10,441 --> 00:15:12,840
At the U.S. Coast Guard Aviation Training Center

296
00:15:12,940 --> 00:15:16,540
in Mobile, Alabama, pilots and crews learn to fly

297
00:15:16,640 --> 00:15:18,840
and operate one of the most technologically advanced

298
00:15:18,940 --> 00:15:22,380
helicopters in the fleet, the MH-60T Jayhawk.

299
00:15:25,680 --> 00:15:29,500
It's a variant of the Navy's SH-60 Seahawk,

300
00:15:29,600 --> 00:15:31,300
with several key modifications that

301
00:15:31,400 --> 00:15:35,100
allow it to fly in extreme weather to perform water rescues.

302
00:15:35,200 --> 00:15:37,240
It's a very safe aircraft.

303
00:15:37,340 --> 00:15:39,400
I know we can go out in any kind of weather,

304
00:15:39,401 --> 00:15:42,100
kind of mission.

305
00:15:42,200 --> 00:15:44,780
These helicopters are made to take off and fly in winds

306
00:15:44,880 --> 00:15:48,840
that can exceed 100 miles per hour.

307
00:15:48,940 --> 00:15:51,140
Flying in a hurricane can be scary,

308
00:15:51,240 --> 00:15:54,140
but doing it at night can be even scarier.

309
00:15:54,240 --> 00:15:56,880
We have a term, dark and stormy night.

310
00:15:56,980 --> 00:16:01,920
You really have to rely on the capabilities of the helicopter

311
00:16:02,020 --> 00:16:05,120
to a certain extent, because nighttime over the ocean,

312
00:16:05,220 --> 00:16:08,400
if there's no illumination out there whatsoever,

313
00:16:08,401 --> 00:16:10,740
It's like flying in a closet.

314
00:16:10,741 --> 00:16:12,240
Survivors coming up.

315
00:16:12,241 --> 00:16:14,740
Pilots must rely on the helicopter's GPS

316
00:16:14,741 --> 00:16:17,400
and infrared systems to show them where they are

317
00:16:17,401 --> 00:16:19,240
and how close they are to surging waves.

318
00:16:21,240 --> 00:16:24,780
The typical Jayhawk crew consists of two pilots,

319
00:16:24,781 --> 00:16:28,520
a rescue swimmer, and a flight mechanic

320
00:16:28,521 --> 00:16:30,560
that operates the hoist during a rescue

321
00:16:30,561 --> 00:16:33,060
and maintains the rear cabin during operations.

322
00:16:33,061 --> 00:16:33,761
operations.

323
00:16:36,960 --> 00:16:39,060
The Jayhawk conducts a number of missions,

324
00:16:39,160 --> 00:16:41,840
including security patrols and disaster relief.

325
00:16:41,940 --> 00:16:44,280
But it's primarily responsible for search and rescue

326
00:16:44,380 --> 00:16:45,380
missions.

327
00:16:45,480 --> 00:16:47,540
Modern Marvels joined this training exercise

328
00:16:47,640 --> 00:16:49,040
in the Gulf of Mexico.

329
00:16:49,140 --> 00:16:51,380
Guys, we've been to burning you go searching for a person

330
00:16:51,480 --> 00:16:52,540
in the water.

331
00:16:52,640 --> 00:16:55,520
We want to show up on scene and do a sector search

332
00:16:55,620 --> 00:16:59,380
and stand by to recover anybody we find in the water.

333
00:16:59,480 --> 00:17:01,820
After the command center receives a distress call,

334
00:17:01,821 --> 00:17:04,320
it dispatches a rescue helicopter.

335
00:17:04,321 --> 00:17:06,860
It will then either give the helicopter exact coordinates

336
00:17:06,861 --> 00:17:11,060
of the ship in danger or its last known coordinates.

337
00:17:11,061 --> 00:17:13,160
The primary job of myself as a pilot

338
00:17:13,161 --> 00:17:14,960
is to get the aircraft on scene.

339
00:17:14,961 --> 00:17:17,900
Once we show up on scene, the avionics system

340
00:17:17,901 --> 00:17:20,780
and the glass cockpit here in this new helicopter

341
00:17:20,781 --> 00:17:23,280
will actually fly the search pattern for me.

342
00:17:23,281 --> 00:17:25,820
And so it gives me the ability to use my night vision goggles

343
00:17:25,821 --> 00:17:29,440
to look out the window and actually search for the survivor

344
00:17:29,441 --> 00:17:31,840
or the subject vessel that we might be looking for.

345
00:17:31,841 --> 00:17:35,080
Roger, looks like we found our survivor there at 2 o'clock.

346
00:17:35,081 --> 00:17:36,320
Let's go ahead and do a rescue check

347
00:17:36,321 --> 00:17:39,680
with part two for free fall to fly with the rescue swimmer.

348
00:17:39,681 --> 00:17:41,680
Once the crew spots the distressed ship,

349
00:17:41,681 --> 00:17:46,400
or floating survivor, the rescue swimmer gets the call.

350
00:17:46,401 --> 00:17:49,800
First thing that'll happen is they'll survey the scene.

351
00:17:49,801 --> 00:17:53,600
If the conditions are met, then the swimmer will free fall.

352
00:17:53,601 --> 00:17:56,600
Swimmer's late.

353
00:17:56,601 --> 00:17:59,040
Once the swimmer free falls, they'll go down to the victim

354
00:17:59,041 --> 00:18:01,500
and based upon their evaluation,

355
00:18:01,940 --> 00:18:03,660
they'll give hand signals to the flight mech.

356
00:18:03,900 --> 00:18:05,240
We're ready for A-man-2 recovery

357
00:18:05,241 --> 00:18:06,140
as a rescue from a survivor.

358
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:08,720
Unlike its military counterpart,

359
00:18:09,300 --> 00:18:11,500
the Jayhawk is equipped with another modification,

360
00:18:12,160 --> 00:18:14,780
a hoist mounted on the right side of the aircraft

361
00:18:14,781 --> 00:18:16,440
to aid in survivor rescues.

362
00:18:17,900 --> 00:18:20,740
The Coast Guard's Jayhawk also lets the flight mechanic

363
00:18:20,741 --> 00:18:21,780
fine-tune the rescue.

364
00:18:24,080 --> 00:18:27,260
This aircraft has a capability that if I need to,

365
00:18:27,261 --> 00:18:30,260
I can control from the back small movements,

366
00:18:30,261 --> 00:18:33,940
either left, right, forward or aft.

367
00:18:33,941 --> 00:18:35,660
At this point, the flight mechanic

368
00:18:35,661 --> 00:18:38,280
takes control of the operation to bring the survivor

369
00:18:38,281 --> 00:18:40,080
on board the helicopter.

370
00:18:40,081 --> 00:18:41,700
Target set 2 o'clock to get the hoist.

371
00:18:41,701 --> 00:18:42,840
Roger, I'm targeting.

372
00:18:42,841 --> 00:18:45,780
I'm going to hook the hoist hook to the basket,

373
00:18:45,781 --> 00:18:48,440
and then I'm going to send it out the door,

374
00:18:48,441 --> 00:18:50,980
and I'm going to be advising the pilot as it's going down

375
00:18:50,981 --> 00:18:53,960
as to whether it's halfway down or holding

376
00:18:53,961 --> 00:18:55,580
five feet above the water.

377
00:18:55,581 --> 00:18:56,820
Halfway down.

378
00:18:56,821 --> 00:18:59,820
The pilot's going to tell me to direct him to that person,

379
00:18:59,821 --> 00:19:03,420
and I will give commands as far as increments and feet

380
00:19:03,421 --> 00:19:04,220
where to go.

381
00:19:04,221 --> 00:19:06,220
Forward and right to five.

382
00:19:06,221 --> 00:19:07,720
Right to two.

383
00:19:07,721 --> 00:19:09,820
Once we get to that person, I'm going to set the basket

384
00:19:09,821 --> 00:19:10,720
in the water.

385
00:19:10,721 --> 00:19:13,340
I'm going to advise the pilot that they're in and ready

386
00:19:13,341 --> 00:19:14,140
for pickup.

387
00:19:14,141 --> 00:19:16,040
Fire, turn the water on the way up.

388
00:19:16,041 --> 00:19:19,080
At that point, I will hoist the basket,

389
00:19:19,081 --> 00:19:20,240
and they'll be on their way up.

390
00:19:20,241 --> 00:19:22,940
The basket is at the cabin door.

391
00:19:22,941 --> 00:19:24,980
When they get to the door, I'll bring them inside the

392
00:19:24,981 --> 00:19:28,280
The flight mechanic will then send the basket down

393
00:19:28,281 --> 00:19:29,580
to retrieve the swimmer.

394
00:19:32,980 --> 00:19:35,360
Coast Guard crews are responsible for hundreds

395
00:19:35,361 --> 00:19:38,860
of miles of ocean, so fuel can be a major consideration.

396
00:19:38,861 --> 00:19:43,360
The H-60 Jayhawk helicopter maximum load of fuel

397
00:19:43,361 --> 00:19:45,100
is just over 6,000 pounds.

398
00:19:45,101 --> 00:19:48,440
With 6,000 pounds of fuel, you can probably plan on flying

399
00:19:48,441 --> 00:19:49,940
for about five and a half hours.

400
00:19:49,941 --> 00:19:54,040
We can go 300 miles offshore, remain on scene

401
00:19:54,041 --> 00:19:58,480
remain on scene for 30 minutes and return back to where we started.

402
00:19:59,880 --> 00:20:03,380
Sometimes their missions aren't far out at sea, but on land.

403
00:20:04,040 --> 00:20:06,960
When Hurricane Ida hit the Gulf Coast in 2021

404
00:20:06,961 --> 00:20:09,600
and Hurricane Katrina in 2005,

405
00:20:10,460 --> 00:20:12,840
the Coast Guard helicopter crews got the call.

406
00:20:14,540 --> 00:20:16,320
And we showed up on scene in New Orleans,

407
00:20:16,540 --> 00:20:18,360
and we started seeing flickers of light

408
00:20:18,361 --> 00:20:20,760
coming from roofs and windows and stuff,

409
00:20:20,761 --> 00:20:23,420
and those were people that were trapped in their flooded houses.

410
00:20:24,040 --> 00:20:29,040
So we immediately went into a poisoning situation where we started picking people up off the roofs.

411
00:20:29,041 --> 00:20:31,840
Went to the first people we saw, put the basket down.

412
00:20:31,841 --> 00:20:34,440
They usually climbed in the basket, got them in the aircraft,

413
00:20:34,441 --> 00:20:37,840
and then we took them to the nearest safe place that they weren't going to ground.

414
00:20:37,841 --> 00:20:40,640
We unloaded them, went right back into the neighborhood we got them from,

415
00:20:40,641 --> 00:20:42,640
and went to the next house down the line.

416
00:20:42,641 --> 00:20:47,440
We did this all night until daylight, and one day we'd pick up over 100 people,

417
00:20:47,441 --> 00:20:51,640
and I did that for the first four days straight over in New Orleans.

418
00:20:51,641 --> 00:20:54,840
The Coast Guard led the way, as military and civilian

419
00:20:54,940 --> 00:20:57,040
helicopters saved thousands of lives.

420
00:20:57,140 --> 00:21:00,740
The sky was literally filled with helicopters everywhere.

421
00:21:03,480 --> 00:21:05,980
The US Coast Guard effectively uses helicopters

422
00:21:06,080 --> 00:21:09,860
to save people from water, but one helicopter saves people

423
00:21:09,960 --> 00:21:12,020
with water.

424
00:21:12,120 --> 00:21:15,360
It's called the Ericsson Aircrane, and with its unique

425
00:21:15,460 --> 00:21:18,360
dragonfly appearance, it's never mistaken for any other

426
00:21:18,460 --> 00:21:19,400
helicopter.

427
00:21:21,640 --> 00:21:25,380
The air crane is built for one thing, heavy lifting.

428
00:21:25,480 --> 00:21:28,740
It has two turbine engines that provide 9,000 horsepower

429
00:21:28,840 --> 00:21:33,860
to the shaft, allowing the 19,500-pound helicopter

430
00:21:33,960 --> 00:21:38,320
to carry an additional 20,000 pounds.

431
00:21:38,420 --> 00:21:41,120
This capability makes the air crane a perfect tool

432
00:21:41,220 --> 00:21:45,000
for fighting large forest and brush fires.

433
00:21:45,100 --> 00:21:49,500
The air crane can be fitted with a 2,650-gallon tank

434
00:21:49,501 --> 00:21:52,500
with a suctioning device called a ram scoop hydrofoil

435
00:21:52,501 --> 00:21:56,000
that can fill the tank in less than 40 seconds.

436
00:21:56,001 --> 00:22:00,000
This tank empties over a 265,000-square-foot area

437
00:22:00,001 --> 00:22:02,500
and can extinguish fires that might take ground crews

438
00:22:02,501 --> 00:22:04,500
precious hours to control and put out.

439
00:22:09,500 --> 00:22:12,500
This is the helicopter that dominated the civilian market

440
00:22:12,501 --> 00:22:14,500
for nearly four decades.

441
00:22:14,501 --> 00:22:16,500
It's called the Bell Jet Ranger.

442
00:22:16,501 --> 00:22:19,500
The Bell helicopter was the first helicopter

443
00:22:19,600 --> 00:22:23,300
that really was successful in the commercial market,

444
00:22:23,400 --> 00:22:25,740
and the ones that you see on TV, for instance,

445
00:22:25,840 --> 00:22:26,940
in old movies.

446
00:22:29,440 --> 00:22:32,380
Born in 1967, it soon became a favorite

447
00:22:32,480 --> 00:22:34,280
of police departments, news stations,

448
00:22:34,380 --> 00:22:37,380
and wealthy businessmen all over the world.

449
00:22:37,480 --> 00:22:39,620
But these jet turbine-driven helicopters

450
00:22:39,720 --> 00:22:42,300
came with a high price tag.

451
00:22:42,301 --> 00:22:47,600
By 1971, they cost nearly $115,000, the equivalent of

452
00:22:47,601 --> 00:22:51,740
nearly $800,000 today.

453
00:22:51,741 --> 00:22:54,840
Ex-PEL engineer Frank Robinson decided he could make a

454
00:22:54,841 --> 00:22:57,740
helicopter that would be cheaper to fly and maintain.

455
00:22:57,741 --> 00:23:01,960
In 1979, Robinson helicopters started to manufacture the

456
00:23:01,961 --> 00:23:05,800
two-seat R22, selling them for the same price as a high-end

457
00:23:05,801 --> 00:23:10,700
luxury car, about $40,000.

458
00:23:10,701 --> 00:23:15,100
Then, in 1992, Robinson began selling the R44,

459
00:23:15,200 --> 00:23:20,140
a four-seat version, for $235,000.

460
00:23:20,240 --> 00:23:24,320
Robinson started with a cheaper, more efficient engine.

461
00:23:24,420 --> 00:23:27,680
The R22 model uses a four-cylinder piston engine

462
00:23:27,780 --> 00:23:32,420
instead of a jet turbine, while the larger R44

463
00:23:32,520 --> 00:23:35,160
uses a six-cylinder engine.

464
00:23:35,260 --> 00:23:38,420
That is by far the most practical type of engine

465
00:23:38,421 --> 00:23:41,760
of engine to use in a small light helicopter like ours

466
00:23:41,860 --> 00:23:46,660
because it burns a lot less fuel than a turbine.

467
00:23:46,760 --> 00:23:48,860
Though less powerful than a turbine engine,

468
00:23:48,960 --> 00:23:52,260
the piston engine still pushes the R-22 to a top speed

469
00:23:52,360 --> 00:23:58,480
of 117 miles per hour and a range of almost 300 miles.

470
00:23:58,580 --> 00:24:00,940
The R-44 has even better performance,

471
00:24:01,040 --> 00:24:03,940
with a top speed of 125 miles per hour

472
00:24:04,040 --> 00:24:08,420
and a range of almost 350 miles.

473
00:24:08,421 --> 00:24:13,120
Finally, Robinson also introduced the R66 in 2007,

474
00:24:13,121 --> 00:24:18,800
which seats five and has a top speed of 160 miles per hour.

475
00:24:18,801 --> 00:24:22,660
Well, we've kept the design very simple, very lightweight,

476
00:24:22,661 --> 00:24:26,500
and yet very, very reliable.

477
00:24:26,501 --> 00:24:31,480
The features of our helicopters are designed for efficiency,

478
00:24:31,481 --> 00:24:34,840
for speed, to keep the noise level down.

479
00:24:34,841 --> 00:24:38,180
And what you probably recognize if you see one flying over,

480
00:24:38,440 --> 00:24:39,820
it tells you that it's one of ours.

481
00:24:39,821 --> 00:24:43,020
It has a tall mast.

482
00:24:44,820 --> 00:24:48,880
The rotor is up considerably higher than the rest of the fuselage.

483
00:24:49,160 --> 00:24:52,860
And we do that because it allows us to use a large diameter rotor,

484
00:24:53,160 --> 00:24:56,820
which is more efficient than a smaller diameter rotor.

485
00:24:57,460 --> 00:25:00,840
The larger mast allows the blades to extend over the aft rotor.

486
00:25:02,860 --> 00:25:04,840
But before they fly...

487
00:25:04,841 --> 00:25:09,840
They all start out as nuts and bolts and sheet metal.

488
00:25:09,841 --> 00:25:11,840
The raw materials come in one door,

489
00:25:11,841 --> 00:25:15,840
and they end up going out another door as a finished flying

490
00:25:15,841 --> 00:25:16,840
helicopter.

491
00:25:18,840 --> 00:25:21,840
Nearly every part of every Robinson helicopter

492
00:25:21,841 --> 00:25:24,840
is made in-house and entirely built by hand.

493
00:25:26,840 --> 00:25:29,840
That process starts with cutting and shaping the metal

494
00:25:29,841 --> 00:25:32,840
into specific sizes to be sent to the sub-assemblies.

495
00:25:32,841 --> 00:25:36,580
This is where all the parts are constructed before being sent

496
00:25:36,581 --> 00:25:38,480
to final assembly.

497
00:25:38,481 --> 00:25:43,080
Here, parts like the tail boom, rotor blades, cabin,

498
00:25:43,081 --> 00:25:47,080
and landing skids are assembled into a complete helicopter shop.

499
00:25:47,081 --> 00:25:51,600
And then the helicopter is moved on its landing gear,

500
00:25:51,601 --> 00:25:55,600
on down the assembly line, getting its electrical harness

501
00:25:55,601 --> 00:26:01,600
installed, its gearbox is put in, and the engine is installed.

502
00:26:01,601 --> 00:26:05,100
After the helicopter is completely assembled,

503
00:26:05,200 --> 00:26:07,740
it's painted, flight tested, and ready for the customer

504
00:26:07,840 --> 00:26:10,100
to fly at home.

505
00:26:10,200 --> 00:26:12,740
Of course, they cost a little more now.

506
00:26:12,840 --> 00:26:16,840
The R-22 sells for about $300,000.

507
00:26:16,940 --> 00:26:20,720
And the R-44 for nearly $500,000.

508
00:26:20,820 --> 00:26:26,800
And the R-66 will set you back over $900,000.

509
00:26:26,900 --> 00:26:30,760
One agency that realizes the value of a Robinson helicopter

510
00:26:30,761 --> 00:26:35,000
helicopter is the Almonte, California Police Department.

511
