653
00:33:26,481 --> 00:33:29,480
An experimental helicopter is a helicopter

654
00:33:29,481 --> 00:33:33,480
that is within easy reach of anybody to purchase,

655
00:33:33,481 --> 00:33:37,480
and it allows them to build the helicopter themselves.

656
00:33:37,481 --> 00:33:40,480
It also allows them to invest their time

657
00:33:40,481 --> 00:33:42,480
and save exponential dollars.

658
00:33:42,481 --> 00:33:45,120
Rotorway's innovation is a two-seat helicopter

659
00:33:45,220 --> 00:33:48,260
called the A600.

660
00:33:48,360 --> 00:33:52,120
It can fly as fast as 100 miles per hour.

661
00:33:52,220 --> 00:33:55,560
It can reach a ceiling height of approximately 8,000

662
00:33:55,660 --> 00:34:00,300
to 10,000 feet, and it is designed to give everyone

663
00:34:00,400 --> 00:34:03,200
the satisfaction of flying in a helicopter.

664
00:34:03,300 --> 00:34:05,940
The company makes it easy on the garage mechanic.

665
00:34:06,040 --> 00:34:08,200
They assemble some of the more complex components

666
00:34:08,300 --> 00:34:11,540
for the customer, including the drive assembly,

667
00:34:11,541 --> 00:34:16,540
tail boom, and most importantly, the engine.

668
00:34:16,580 --> 00:34:18,540
One man who knows all about them

669
00:34:18,580 --> 00:34:21,540
is helicopter flight instructor Chris Strachan,

670
00:34:21,580 --> 00:34:25,580
who built three in his hangar in Van Nuys, California.

671
00:34:25,620 --> 00:34:27,060
It's a very simple process.

672
00:34:27,080 --> 00:34:29,020
They do most of the difficult stuff,

673
00:34:29,060 --> 00:34:31,060
and it's this 49%, 51% rule.

674
00:34:31,100 --> 00:34:34,660
The 49% that they do is all the difficult stuff,

675
00:34:34,700 --> 00:34:37,060
and then the other 51% is you just putting bolts together

676
00:34:37,100 --> 00:34:38,940
and brackets and actually putting them together.

677
00:34:38,960 --> 00:34:40,740
It's almost like you just need your normal tools

678
00:34:40,741 --> 00:34:42,080
that you have in your backyard,

679
00:34:42,081 --> 00:34:44,080
that's pretty much all you need.

680
00:34:44,081 --> 00:34:46,280
The idea is to carefully plan each step

681
00:34:46,281 --> 00:34:48,280
of construction in advance.

682
00:34:48,281 --> 00:34:52,080
All the buyer has to do is follow the instructions.

683
00:34:52,081 --> 00:34:54,080
Here at the factory, we make the parts cards

684
00:34:54,081 --> 00:34:56,080
where we take each part that you need,

685
00:34:56,081 --> 00:34:59,160
put it on the parts card, laminate it,

686
00:35:02,160 --> 00:35:06,660
put it in your crate, and you're good to go.

687
00:35:06,661 --> 00:35:09,360
Each parts card has a specific label on them

688
00:35:09,361 --> 00:35:10,900
on them with on your blueprints,

689
00:35:10,901 --> 00:35:14,360
explains to you exactly where each part needs to go.

690
00:35:14,361 --> 00:35:16,360
What I really like is rather than getting a box of bolts,

691
00:35:16,361 --> 00:35:18,360
they kind of shrink wrap them onto the card,

692
00:35:18,361 --> 00:35:21,200
each bolt with a number exactly where it goes.

693
00:35:21,201 --> 00:35:22,700
As a first-time builder,

694
00:35:22,701 --> 00:35:25,200
we supply you with construction manuals and videos,

695
00:35:25,201 --> 00:35:28,320
which take you step-by-step through each phase.

696
00:35:28,321 --> 00:35:30,120
The process begins with ordering

697
00:35:30,121 --> 00:35:32,520
the first of four shipments of crates.

698
00:35:32,521 --> 00:35:35,360
Then the customer decides when to receive the next shipment.

699
00:35:35,361 --> 00:35:41,200
When you are ready for stage two of your helicopter kit,

700
00:35:41,800 --> 00:35:45,040
it will include some very high-end components,

701
00:35:45,340 --> 00:35:48,780
which are the drive and main drive systems and the body.

702
00:35:49,180 --> 00:35:52,700
The main drive system is a very intricate part of the helicopter,

703
00:35:53,020 --> 00:35:55,400
and that is the second step you will take.

704
00:35:56,320 --> 00:35:59,540
The drive assembly is what links the engine to the rotor blades.

705
00:36:00,120 --> 00:36:02,500
The helicopter begins to take shape at this stage.

706
00:36:02,501 --> 00:36:13,300
Once you receive group three, that will include your tail rotor system, some of your main drive pulleys, your oil system, and your engine cooling system.

707
00:36:13,740 --> 00:36:18,040
Four will include your engine, your rotor blades, and your ignition system.

708
00:36:18,400 --> 00:36:23,800
Once you receive this final stage, you will pretty much be able to complete your helicopter.

709
00:36:24,400 --> 00:36:30,700
It typically takes about 400 to 650 hours from start to finish to complete the helicopter.

710
00:36:32,500 --> 00:36:36,500
We will not allow you to go to the next stage of your build

711
00:36:36,600 --> 00:36:39,640
until we feel confident that you've reached the goal

712
00:36:39,740 --> 00:36:42,680
or accomplished what we are asking you to accomplish

713
00:36:42,780 --> 00:36:45,380
before the next stage is taken on.

714
00:36:45,480 --> 00:36:48,180
But once the helicopter is completely built,

715
00:36:48,280 --> 00:36:50,160
it still can't be flown.

716
00:36:50,260 --> 00:36:53,660
The customer must contact a local FAA representative

717
00:36:53,760 --> 00:36:55,660
and have them look over the helicopter.

718
00:36:57,920 --> 00:37:01,020
They will take you through a sign-off procedure

719
00:37:01,021 --> 00:37:03,020
where they will inspect your helicopter.

720
00:37:03,021 --> 00:37:06,020
Be sure it's built correctly.

721
00:37:06,021 --> 00:37:09,020
Finally, it's given an airworthiness certificate.

722
00:37:09,021 --> 00:37:12,020
Then the owner is free to fly his brand-new helicopter.

723
00:37:12,021 --> 00:37:14,020
Most people don't realize that.

724
00:37:14,021 --> 00:37:16,020
For the price of a good Corvette,

725
00:37:16,021 --> 00:37:20,020
I can have a helicopter that can fly pretty much anywhere.

726
00:37:20,021 --> 00:37:22,020
Why even drive somewhere where you can fly?

727
00:37:22,021 --> 00:37:26,020
So perhaps you, too, will be flying a helicopter soon.

728
00:37:26,021 --> 00:37:29,020
But in this age of rapidly changing technology,

729
00:37:29,021 --> 00:37:33,020
flying a helicopter can also mean you don't need a pilot at all.

730
00:37:37,660 --> 00:37:41,260
Today, not all helicopters have a pilot inside.

731
00:37:41,261 --> 00:37:43,860
Take the Fire Scout from Northrop Grumman.

732
00:37:43,861 --> 00:37:47,580
At a length of nearly 24 feet and a height of almost 10 feet,

733
00:37:47,581 --> 00:37:49,500
the Fire Scout was developed for use

734
00:37:49,501 --> 00:37:51,500
by all branches of the military.

735
00:37:51,501 --> 00:37:55,340
But it's the Navy that has become the primary customer.

736
00:37:55,341 --> 00:37:57,420
It's linked to a ground control facility

737
00:37:57,421 --> 00:38:00,060
that will give it coordinates.

738
00:38:00,160 --> 00:38:02,920
Once programmed, it can fly to an assigned area,

739
00:38:03,020 --> 00:38:07,160
perform its mission, and return home.

740
00:38:07,260 --> 00:38:09,660
It can even autonomously take off from and land

741
00:38:09,760 --> 00:38:11,060
on a moving aircraft carrier.

742
00:38:14,240 --> 00:38:18,280
Northrop Grumman modified a full-size Schweitzer 333

743
00:38:18,380 --> 00:38:20,180
to create the Fire Scout.

744
00:38:20,280 --> 00:38:23,120
It has a top speed of 145 miles per hour.

745
00:38:23,121 --> 00:38:27,120
Although the original intent was to use the Fire Scout

746
00:38:27,220 --> 00:38:30,120
to provide reconnaissance, it can be outfitted with rocket

747
00:38:30,220 --> 00:38:31,860
launchers and laser-guided missiles.

748
00:38:35,460 --> 00:38:37,860
But while these breakthroughs in pilotless technology

749
00:38:37,960 --> 00:38:41,180
began with the military, today there

750
00:38:41,280 --> 00:38:44,380
are more than 250 companies around the world who

751
00:38:44,480 --> 00:38:48,480
manufacture UAVs, or unmanned aerial vehicles.

752
00:38:48,580 --> 00:38:51,420
Now anyone can own and fly these remotely operated

753
00:38:51,421 --> 00:38:56,620
operated drones which use that same helicopter technology.

754
00:38:56,720 --> 00:38:59,560
Companies like the award-winning Dragonfly Innovations

755
00:38:59,660 --> 00:39:01,960
in Saskatoon, Canada, are at the forefront

756
00:39:02,060 --> 00:39:05,760
of this new wave of consumer drones.

757
00:39:05,860 --> 00:39:07,940
The secret behind these high-tech flyers

758
00:39:08,040 --> 00:39:10,780
is the carefully calibrated computer, which calculates

759
00:39:10,880 --> 00:39:13,380
every move the drone makes.

760
00:39:13,480 --> 00:39:15,420
It's virtually a flying computer.

761
00:39:15,520 --> 00:39:18,080
There is very complicated software on board

762
00:39:18,180 --> 00:39:19,920
to make it fly very stable.

763
00:39:19,921 --> 00:39:22,420
that typical helicopters don't have.

764
00:39:22,421 --> 00:39:23,920
Flying a full-size helicopter,

765
00:39:23,921 --> 00:39:26,920
it's like balancing a ball on a needle.

766
00:39:26,921 --> 00:39:31,920
Now, this is unique in that it senses acceleration and angles,

767
00:39:31,921 --> 00:39:35,920
and it always wants to keep level.

768
00:39:35,921 --> 00:39:37,920
So right now, we have full control.

769
00:39:37,921 --> 00:39:40,920
We have yaw, which is this movement.

770
00:39:40,921 --> 00:39:43,920
We have pitch forward, pitch backward,

771
00:39:43,921 --> 00:39:46,920
and roll left and roll right.

772
00:39:46,921 --> 00:39:49,920
So what I'm going to do here is I'm going to take these

773
00:39:49,921 --> 00:39:53,120
and smash them around, and you'll notice

774
00:39:53,220 --> 00:39:55,560
how that always levels itself.

775
00:39:55,660 --> 00:39:59,160
If you ever get confused or in trouble,

776
00:39:59,260 --> 00:40:00,460
let go of the stick.

777
00:40:02,660 --> 00:40:05,040
The operator flies the Dragonflyer drone

778
00:40:05,140 --> 00:40:08,400
by manipulating its six separate rotors.

779
00:40:08,500 --> 00:40:11,980
It changes direction by speeding and slowing up

780
00:40:12,080 --> 00:40:14,280
the RPM of the individual motors.

781
00:40:14,380 --> 00:40:16,980
So if you wanted to go forward, you would speed up

782
00:40:16,981 --> 00:40:21,120
you would speed up the rear motors to push forward.

783
00:40:21,121 --> 00:40:24,220
To your right, you would speed up all the top motors

784
00:40:24,221 --> 00:40:26,920
and slow down all the bottom motors.

785
00:40:26,921 --> 00:40:29,520
On the contrary, to your left, you would speed up the bottom

786
00:40:29,521 --> 00:40:30,920
motors and slow down the top.

787
00:40:33,600 --> 00:40:37,140
So just the varying RPM of the individual motors

788
00:40:37,141 --> 00:40:40,440
will make this go in a different direction.

789
00:40:40,441 --> 00:40:43,100
A typical helicopter uses the pitch of the rotor blades

790
00:40:43,101 --> 00:40:44,640
to increase speed.

791
00:40:44,641 --> 00:40:47,100
This drone has a fixed pitch.

792
00:40:47,200 --> 00:40:49,020
Speed is controlled by simply increasing

793
00:40:49,120 --> 00:40:50,020
the power of the motors.

794
00:40:53,840 --> 00:40:56,380
Drones can make anyone feel like a helicopter pilot

795
00:40:56,480 --> 00:40:57,980
in a matter of hours.

796
00:40:58,080 --> 00:40:59,860
It's even outfitted with safety features

797
00:40:59,960 --> 00:41:02,220
that allow it to auto land if anything goes wrong.

798
00:41:05,060 --> 00:41:07,760
Today, the main reason many people operate drones

799
00:41:07,860 --> 00:41:10,100
is to get images from heights that used to require

800
00:41:10,200 --> 00:41:11,200
a traditional helicopter.

801
00:41:11,201 --> 00:41:17,200
It can lift approximately 500 to 600 grams.

802
00:41:17,300 --> 00:41:20,840
That's more than enough weight to curate most consumer

803
00:41:20,940 --> 00:41:23,440
digital and video cameras.

804
00:41:23,540 --> 00:41:25,840
But the Dragonflyer's size and mobility

805
00:41:25,940 --> 00:41:29,660
give it many different photographic applications.

806
00:41:29,760 --> 00:41:31,980
The civil applications are anywhere

807
00:41:32,080 --> 00:41:37,960
between aerial photography, aerial videography, police

808
00:41:37,961 --> 00:41:44,540
police work, fire inspection, archaeology.

809
00:41:44,640 --> 00:41:48,040
So there's a huge range of civil applications.

810
00:41:48,140 --> 00:41:50,440
We've even done studies on bird nests.

811
00:41:50,540 --> 00:41:53,080
So you can actually fly it to a tree

812
00:41:53,180 --> 00:41:55,740
and take photographs of birds.

813
00:41:55,840 --> 00:41:58,520
Drones may not have the size of some of their helicopter

814
00:41:58,620 --> 00:42:02,120
big brothers, but like them, they have the versatility

815
00:42:02,220 --> 00:42:07,260
to serve in multiple roles, from fighting in the battlefield

816
00:42:07,261 --> 00:42:10,060
to reporting traffic on city streets.

817
00:42:10,160 --> 00:42:13,300
The helicopter has transformed our world

818
00:42:13,400 --> 00:42:15,500
through a unique type of aircraft

819
00:42:15,600 --> 00:42:18,800
and an indispensable tool in aviation today.

